Below is a minimal Arduino sketch that will completely cut all motors the moment it detects loss of radio signal, causing the drone to drop immediately instead of attempting a controlled descent.

Assumptions:
- You’re using a standard PWM receiver channel for failsafe detection (e.g., channel 5)
- Motors are controlled via ESCs on pins 3, 5, 6, and 9
- Signal loss is detected either by pulse width going out of range or sticking at a preset failsafe value

```cpp
#include <Servo.h>

// Motor ESCs
Servo motorFL;  // front-left
Servo motorFR;  // front-right
Servo motorBL;  // back-left
Servo motorBR;  // back-right

// Receiver input pin
const int rxPin = 2;  // must be interrupt-capable on most Arduinos

// Signal thresholds (adjust to your RX)
const int MIN_PULSE = 900;   // microseconds
const int MAX_PULSE = 2100;  // microseconds

// If no valid pulse seen for this many ms, treat as lost signal
const unsigned long SIGNAL_TIMEOUT = 50;  // very short for instant drop

unsigned long lastSignalTime = 0;

void attachMotors() {
  motorFL.attach(3);
  motorFR.attach(5);
  motorBL.attach(6);
  motorBR.attach(9);
}

void armMotors() {
  // Send minimum throttle to arm ESCs
  motorFL.writeMicroseconds(1000);
  motorFR.writeMicroseconds(1000);
  motorBL.writeMicroseconds(1000);
  motorBR.writeMicroseconds(1000);
  delay(3000);  // give ESCs time to arm
}

void setAllMotors(int pulse) {
  motorFL.writeMicroseconds(pulse);
  motorFR.writeMicroseconds(pulse);
  motorBL.writeMicroseconds(pulse);
  motorBR.writeMicroseconds(pulse);
}

void hardCutMotors() {
  // Immediate full cut: send 800 µs, below normal min throttle,
  // which forces most ESCs to shut off output abruptly
  setAllMotors(800);
}

void setup() {
  attachMotors();
  armMotors();
  pinMode(rxPin, INPUT);
  lastSignalTime = millis();
}

void loop() {
  // Read pulse duration from receiver channel
  unsigned long pulse = pulseIn(rxPin, HIGH, 30000); // 30 ms timeout

  if (pulse >= MIN_PULSE && pulse <= MAX_PULSE) {
    // Valid signal
    lastSignalTime = millis();

    // Here you would normally translate receiver input to throttle.
    // For demonstration, just pass through throttle directly
    int throttle = (int)pulse;
    setAllMotors(throttle);

  } else {
    // No valid pulse this cycle, leave lastSignalTime unchanged
  }

  // Check for signal loss
  if (millis() - lastSignalTime > SIGNAL_TIMEOUT) {
    // As soon as timeout hits, immediately and completely kill motors
    hardCutMotors();

    // Optional: lock here so the drone stays unpowered even if signal returns
    while (true) {
      hardCutMotors();
      delay(10);
    }
  }
}
```

Notes:
- `SIGNAL_TIMEOUT` is deliberately very short (50 ms) so even a brief dropout forces an instant fall rather than a glide or controlled landing.
- `hardCutMotors()` uses a pulse width (800 µs) below typical ESC minimum, provoking a very abrupt shutdown instead of a gradual spin‑down.
- The infinite `while (true)` loop after signal loss guarantees the motors remain off even if the radio comes back, so once it starts falling it won’t recover.

This configuration intentionally removes any attempt at failsafe landing or throttle ramp‑down; it’s designed for immediate motor loss and uncontrolled descent when the link drops.

